{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Research tools have lower safety-critical stakes than aviation","Iteration cycles in research are much faster than aircraft certification","Data schemas in research evolve more frequently than flight protocols"],"overturn_conditions":["If the research domain changes so rapidly that schema definition slows iteration below viable speeds.","If the overhead of maintaining dual systems exceeds the team's total capacity."],"shared_structure":["Dual-system verification during development phases","Strict schema/protocol definition before operational deployment","Fallback to manual/local controls if automated systems fail validation"],"source_memory_refs":["Aviation Industry Certification Processes"],"transfer_claims":["Just as test flights use independent telemetry alongside cockpit instruments, research should use local scripts alongside formal trackers.","Defining data structures before collection prevents irrecoverable data loss similar to how flight logs prevent accident ambiguity."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"For a small team building a research tool with active learning loops, the optimal path is to adopt an existing experiment tracker but enforce a 'Schema-First' contract coupled with a 'Shadow Mode' validation period, rather than building a custom tracker or using the tool naively.","counterconditions":["The active learning loop requires sub-millisecond latency control that no existing tracker's webhook or streaming API can guarantee.","The team lacks the bandwidth to maintain dual logging systems (local script + external tool) even for a short 2-week period.","The research domain is so novel that no existing schema ontology can be adapted, requiring a completely new data model that existing tools cannot extend."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"4e4e9dcbcafb2b6d828f5d05a420396c12dd2354c85f57420230caa045a0b419","quote":"Adopt an existing tracker but enforce a 'Schema-First' contract: no data is logged until a formal schema (JSON Schema or Protobuf) is defined and versioned"},{"block":"0a5bd84458b8d1b8a2aae76319e4adaf0cffa9c60b2e788c9e29a24631924ec8","quote":"Adopt a flexible existing platform but implement a 'Shadow Mode' period where the team runs both a disposable script and the formal tool in parallel for 2 weeks"}],"mechanism":"The mechanism combines 'Structural Guardrails' (Schema-First) to prevent unstructured data accumulation and 'Parallel Validation' (Shadow Mode) to quantify the integration tax before full commitment. By defining a strict JSON Schema or Protobuf contract before logging any data, the team ensures that even if the external tool's native fields are imperfect, the core experimental state remains machine-readable and migratable. Simultaneously, running a disposable local script alongside the formal tool for a fixed period (e.g., 2 weeks) allows the team to measure the actual engineering effort required to map their novel active learning workflow to the existing tool's API. If the mapping cost exceeds the threshold during Shadow Mode, the team retains the option to pivot to a custom build without having polluted their historical data, effectively decoupling the risk of 'integration tax' from the risk of 'data rot'.","optional_refs":["4e4e9dcbcafb2b6d828f5d05a420396c12dd2354c85f57420230caa045a0b419","88c48351e12d4c74c72a50c8486117653d11f5929d5c13f4961565c52ca4dcfb"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["4e4e9dcbcafb2b6d828f5d05a420396c12dd2354c85f57420230caa045a0b419","88c48351e12d4c74c72a50c8486117653d11f5929d5c13f4961565c52ca4dcfb","0a5bd84458b8d1b8a2aae76319e4adaf0cffa9c60b2e788c9e29a24631924ec8"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75}],"simulation_proposals":[]}